Never store plain text passwords. Use password hashing with salt. Best practices:

1) Use bcrypt (Spring Security default):
@Configuration
public class SecurityConfig {
  @Bean
  public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
    return new BCryptPasswordEncoder(12); // strength 12
  }
}

@Service
public class UserService {
  @Autowired
  private PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;

  public void createUser(String username, String password) {
    String hashedPassword = passwordEncoder.encode(password);
    // $2a$12$R9h/cIPz0gi.URNNX3kh2O (60 chars)
    user.setPassword(hashedPassword);
    userRepo.save(user);
  }

  public boolean validatePassword(String rawPassword, String hashedPassword) {
    return passwordEncoder.matches(rawPassword, hashedPassword);
  }
}

2) Or use Argon2 (newer, more resistant to GPU attacks):
@Bean
public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
  return new Argon2PasswordEncoder(16, 32, 1, 65536, 3);
}

3) Password requirements:
- Minimum 12 characters
- Complexity rules (upper, lower, digit, special)
- No common passwords (use password dictionary check)
- Rate limiting on login (prevent brute force)

4) Password validation:
@Component
public class PasswordValidator {
  private static final Pattern PASSWORD_PATTERN = 
    Pattern.compile("^(?=.*[a-z])(?=.*[A-Z])(?=.*\\d)(?=.*[@$!%*?&])[A-Za-z\\d@$!%*?&]{12,}$");

  public boolean isValid(String password) {
    return PASSWORD_PATTERN.matcher(password).matches() &&
           !isCommonPassword(password);
  }
}

5) Additional security:
- MFA (Multi-Factor Authentication)
- Account lockout after failed attempts
- Password expiration policy (controversial, may reduce security)
- Password history (prevent reuse)

Never: MD5, SHA1, plain SHA-256 (too fast, vulnerable to rainbow tables). Always: bcrypt, Argon2, PBKDF2 with salt.
